{
  "dom_id": "step-e2565",
  "name": "Example: Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points",
  "problem": "Let $C,$ $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E.$ If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182164/assets/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =CD+DE \\\\ 8 & =6+DE \\\\ 8-6 & =DE \\\\ 2 & =DE\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $DE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}$.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
